import{_ as t,o as r,c as i,a3 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"jnk’s Hypotheses about the Underlying Logic of Several Issues","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"jnk 对一些问题的底层逻辑推测","archivedAt":"2026-11-23","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9.md"}'),s={name:"jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues.md"};function n(o,e,l,h,p,d){return r(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="jnk-s-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues" tabindex="-1">jnk’s Hypotheses about the Underlying Logic of Several Issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#jnk-s-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;jnk’s Hypotheses about the Underlying Logic of Several Issues&quot;">​</a></h1><p class="page-updated" data-update-status="recorded"><span>Content updated: </span><a href="/endfield-skydoc/changelog/capture-2026-11-23T06-44-45-722Z-4358a738" title="Date when the mirror captured this page’s latest content change (China Standard Time), not the author’s edit time. View the update record." aria-label="Content updated: 2026-11-23. Date when the mirror captured this page’s latest content change (China Standard Time), not the author’s edit time. View the update record."><time datetime="2026-11-23T06:44:47.018Z">2026-11-23</time></a><span class="page-updated-zone"> (China Standard Time)</span></p><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>BV12zSfBeEkP<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV12zSfBeEkP/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click&amp;vd_source=a5b44235b65b539588d57101d1b0a250" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Endfield&#39;s Mysterious Mechanics</a></p><p>Normally, when a factory output is flush against a converger, more logistics components mean lower priority. But calculating terminal order—a certain ordering of logistics components connected to downstream factory inputs, treating the final component as a factory if none is downstream—is impossible with only one component. That case has special rules depending on the connection order between the component and upstream factory, and between that factory and its input. Moving/replacing the component and factory input to change their order visibly changes output priority.</p><p>BV1yVDjBLEYe<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1yVDjBLEYe/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click&amp;vd_source=a5b44235b65b539588d57101d1b0a250" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Endfield Splitting and Merging Issues</a></p><p>For the splitter and converger in this layout, you can ignore the first pair and feed two full belts directly into the second converger and splitter to see a similar effect. The damping or level (roughly the number of downstream logistics components) can be equal or unequal. If the end belt on the splitter–belt–factory route is connected first (an informal way to describe endpoint order), that route determines the splitter&#39;s damping. It then matches the converger&#39;s, and whichever was placed first is processed first. Otherwise the other route determines it. That route runs upstream of the converger, so the damping is higher and the splitter is processed later.</p><p>With equal damping, a splitter seeing a converger and belt downstream checks whether the converger&#39;s output was connected first. If so, it tries to make the converger output before itself, freeing space for normal splitting. When the converger computes first, the splitter has lower damping than the converger&#39;s other input and computes first, gaining priority and splitting normally.<br> With unequal damping, normal merge priority applies: the upstream component placed first has priority.<br> Different outcomes can be obtained by replacing the terminal factory and changing placement order of components flush against the converger&#39;s upstream and downstream sides.</p><p>BV1TJQKBxEph<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1TJQKBxEph/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click&amp;vd_source=a5b44235b65b539588d57101d1b0a250" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chishi Tech: One-Fifth Splitting</a></p><p>This uses the preceding video&#39;s principle. Output depends on connection order between splitter and converger and between converger and downstream. Changing placement order of the downstream belt and upstream splitter changes it. Here is what occurs between splitter and converger in the two cases.<br> Belt placed later: the splitter does not first try to make the converger output. An item remains in the converger, so the splitter cannot output to it; one recirculating item stalls two splitter items.<br> Belt placed earlier: splitter output makes the converger output first, allowing normal splitting.</p><p>BV1KxdGBJETp<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1KxdGBJETp/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chishi Tech: Splitter Failure</a></p><p>Yes, it involves timing. The two cases have different terminal orders. Among components ordered for the same terminal factory, non-splitters merge into the foremost entry, also the starting point of downstream-to-upstream BFS. This produces different damping values, which are not overwritten. In the left case, the right Protocol Stash was presumably placed first, so the splitter, upstream of the right-hand belt, computes after it. The belt has already output when the splitter does, allowing normal splitting. In the right case, there is only one terminal; all belts simultaneously start BFS. The splitter receives lower damping than the right-hand belt and tries output first, while that belt still holds an item and blocks it.</p><p>BV1FJ546LEg1<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1FJ546LEg1/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Endfield: Pipe Length Affects Splitting</a></p><p>My working hypothesis is that when the upstream component is processed first and the downstream one fills, the upstream output lags by one minimum spacing unit, producing something like the 4n-items-per-1-s-hitch pattern. The liquid split at the first splitter is 0111. When the first two of the three consecutive drops fill the next pipe, the last drop and subsequent drops are held back by one minimum spacing unit. This resembles what you show in your latest video BV1nG346uEVg<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1nG346uEVg/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click&amp;vd_source=a5b44235b65b539588d57101d1b0a250" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Endfield: Splitter Stuttering Bug</a>. For belts, minimum spacing is 1/8 tile and the minimum visible spacing is 1/2 tile. With a one-tile downstream belt, once it fills the second item&#39;s output falls back by 1/8, and only after 4 obstructions across 4 items does the 1/2-tile gap become visible. To test this, give the output merge priority. The extra 1/8 tile delay per item should still let the lower-priority input enter. For a longer belt, one lower-priority item should merge after every several items.</p><p>BV1heGW6nE18<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1heGW6nE18/?spm_id_from=333.337.search-card.all.click&amp;vd_source=a5b44235b65b539588d57101d1b0a250" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chishi Tech: A Study of Endfield Splitter and Converger Mechanics</a></p><p>This is exactly the verification described in the first paragraph.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
